# Risk Register {#risk-register}

## RM — Model & technical correctness {.section}

### RM1: Containment/graph duality has no clean answer {.risk #rm1} ^rm1
likelihood:: L
impact:: H
phase:: B (Stage 4)
status:: resolved
signal:: model needs two out-of-sync structures

One graph; document = containment spanning-tree walked in order (D-016)

### RM2: Block-granularity bet wrong — knowledge routinely needs sub-block structure {.risk #rm2} ^rm2
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: reference impl
status:: watch
signal:: promotion is the rule, not the exception

Promotion of spans to nodes on demand (Stage 4 §3.1, D-015); revisit if churn dominates

### RM3: Promotion churn — agents over-promote spans → node explosion, id churn {.risk #rm3} ^rm3
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: C/impl (Stage 8)
status:: open
signal:: node count ≫ blocks; id turnover high

Promotion policy + demotion/GC of unreferenced promoted nodes (Stage 8)

### RM4: Anchor stability — tracking a link's position inside an edited block reintroduces intra-text position tracking {.risk #rm4} ^rm4
likelihood:: H
impact:: M
phase:: C (Stage 8/9)
status:: open
signal:: edits break/move anchors; merge conflicts on links

Per-character identity à la CRDT for anchors; budget the metadata weight ([versioning-and-merge.md §6](../research/versioning-and-merge.md))

### RM5: Entity-home "dumping ground" — auto-homed inline entities pile up {.risk #rm5} ^rm5
likelihood:: M
impact:: L
phase:: B (Stage 5)
status:: mitigated
signal:: reserved container holds thousands of nodes

Vocabularies declare canonical homes (D-023 schema); projections hide the container (P17)

### RM6: Vocabulary mis-cut — v0 eight are wrong (too few/many) {.risk #rm6} ^rm6
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: B→impl
status:: mitigated
signal:: `concept` overloaded; a kind unused

Falsifiable inclusion test + additive vocab versioning (D-021, D-025)

### RM7: Reference resolution surprises users (name recurs across scopes) {.risk #rm7} ^rm7
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: B/D
status:: mitigated
signal:: wrong-node links; frequent "unresolved"

Deterministic order + never-guess + diagnostics (D-024); measure ambiguity rates

### RM8: Plain-text-as-truth loses to a DB at scale {.risk #rm8} ^rm8
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: B/C
status:: accepted
signal:: query/write latency unacceptable at 100k nodes

Op-log fallback to live index (R-new-1); see Watch list

### RM9: Expressiveness gap — property graph can't model a needed pattern {.risk #rm9} ^rm9
likelihood:: L
impact:: H
phase:: B (Stage 5)
status:: mitigated
signal:: recurring facts don't fit edges+qualifiers

n-ary/temporal via edge qualifiers (D-020); extension mechanism (D-011) for the rest

### RM10: Determinism breaks — two valid byte-forms of one model → diff churn, cache misses {.risk #rm10} ^rm10
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: C (Stage 9)
status:: resolved
signal:: same edit yields different files across tools

Canonical normal form = line-oriented canonical JSON (JCS/RFC 8785), exactly one byte-string per state (D-041)

### RM19: Canonical-form recomputation cost — re-canonicalizing a large file on every change {.risk #rm19} ^rm19
likelihood:: M
impact:: L
phase:: C (Stage 9)
status:: mitigated
signal:: serialization dominates edit latency at scale

Edits run against the append-only op-log (cheap); canonicalization off-hot-path; incremental canonicalization possible (D-042)

### RM20: Normalization instability — `normalize` not provably idempotent → formatting churn / diff noise {.risk #rm20} ^rm20
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: D (Stage 11)
status:: mitigated
signal:: reformatting a file changes bytes without model change

Idempotence is a conformance requirement (D-051); canonical form is the fixed point; test-corpus coverage

### RM21: Live-view edit ambiguity — a coarse projection can't disambiguate the target model element {.risk #rm21} ^rm21
likelihood:: L
impact:: M
phase:: D (Stage 12)
status:: mitigated
signal:: an edit in a summary view maps to the wrong node

Every projected element carries its id (D-052); unattributable edits rejected as a diagnostic, never guessed

### RM22: Reference-impl-defines-truth drift — implementers treat the reference code as the spec (Markdown's fate) {.risk #rm22} ^rm22
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: E (Stage 13)
status:: mitigated
signal:: conforming = "matches our code," spec ignored

The conformance corpus (input→expected), not the code, is the operational definition; reference impl must itself pass the corpus (D-057)

### RM11: Scale — large KBs blow parse/query/memory {.risk #rm11} ^rm11
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: D (Stage 12)
status:: open
signal:: slow load; whole-file-in-memory required

Partial loading, streaming, section anchors (P6/P14); Stage 12 rendering arch

### RM12: Identity collision — offline id generation collides across replicas {.risk #rm12} ^rm12
likelihood:: L
impact:: H
phase:: C (Stage 9)
status:: resolved
signal:: duplicate ids after merge

`(replica,counter)` / ULID collision-free scheme (D-014, Stage 4 §4)

### RM13: Auto-merge produces valid-but-wrong result {.risk #rm13} ^rm13
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: C (Stage 8)
status:: open
signal:: merged docs semantically corrupt, no conflict raised

Node/edge-level semantic merge; conservative conflict → human review (D-014, Stage 8)

### RM14: An operation violates a model invariant (two homes, dangling edge) {.risk #rm14} ^rm14
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: C (Stage 8)
status:: mitigated
signal:: invariants (§11 Stage 4) breakable by an op sequence

Op set specified closed under the 10 invariants; validation after every op (Stage 8)

### RM15: Traversal determinism depends on a total order over edges {.risk #rm15} ^rm15
likelihood:: L
impact:: M
phase:: B (Stage 6)
status:: mitigated
signal:: same traversal yields different order across tools

Total tie-break cascade (D-029) grounded in totally-orderable `(replica,counter)`/ULID ids (D-014)

### RM16: Filter algebra too weak — a common query can't be expressed {.risk #rm16} ^rm16
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: B (Stage 7)
status:: open
signal:: users hit the predicate ceiling routinely

Saved/composed queries + agent reasoning (AI-selected) as the escape (D-031/D-034); revisit predicate set after dogfooding

### RM17: Query→operation staleness (lost update) — graph changes between read and id-addressed write {.risk #rm17} ^rm17
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: C (Stage 8)
status:: mitigated
signal:: concurrent edits silently clobber each other

Optimistic-concurrency `expect` precondition per op (version/status/value check); violated → rejected + re-query (D-038)

### RM18: LWW silently drops the losing side of a concurrent same-node content/move edit {.risk #rm18} ^rm18
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: C (Stage 8)
status:: open
signal:: a collaborator's block edit vanishes without a conflict

Block-sized nodes bound blast radius; guarded ops (D-038) detect-not-lose; intra-node text CRDT deferred (RM4)

## RH — Human factors & usability {.section}

### RH1: Graph purity destroys writability {.risk #rh1} ^rh1
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: D (Stage 10)
status:: mitigated
signal:: authoring feels like filling a database

Prose-first; edges from prose (P3/P4); writability tests every syntax candidate

### RH2: Humans won't enrich even with agent help (founding bet) {.risk #rh2} ^rh2
likelihood:: H
impact:: H
phase:: reference impl
status:: watch
signal:: enrichment rate flat over time

See Watch list (agent enrichment + per-fact payoff, D-010)

### RH3: Inline typed-link gesture disrupts prose flow {.risk #rh3} ^rh3
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: D (Stage 10)
status:: watch
signal:: authors avoid links; prefer plain text

Writability testing (Stage 10); block-form fallback; D-012 reversal condition armed

### RH4: Cognitive overload — too many kinds/edges to remember {.risk #rh4} ^rh4
likelihood:: L
impact:: M
phase:: D
status:: mitigated
signal:: users can't recall the vocabulary

Thin core, 8 kinds, progressive ladder (D-018, P9)

### RH5: Dialect/canonical split confuses users {.risk #rh5} ^rh5
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: D (Stage 9/10)
status:: watch
signal:: users surprised by normalization

gofmt-style invisible normalization; one obvious canonical form (D-003)

### RH6: IDs/slugs leak into human view and get mis-edited {.risk #rh6} ^rh6
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: D
status:: mitigated
signal:: users hand-edit ids and break links

Tooling hides ids; slugs quarantined; names are the human handle (D-009)

### RH7: Agent enrichment the human didn't intend erodes trust {.risk #rh7} ^rh7
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: impl
status:: mitigated
signal:: users disable enrichment; distrust the graph

Provenance segregation (`inferred` vs `asserted`, D-019); human-confirm loop

### RH8: Attribute/inline-field ceremony (`{.task}`, `key::`) still feels like filling a database {.risk #rh8} ^rh8
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: D (Stage 10)
status:: open
signal:: authors write plain prose, skip all structure

Everything optional (L0 prose valid); agent adds most structure (D-010); test hand-authoring cost; Candidate A minimizes marks

## RA — AI / LLM-specific {.section}

### RA1: LLMs can't reliably read/write .sarib {.risk #ra1} ^ra1
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: impl
status:: watch
signal:: round-trip/edit accuracy poor across models

Markdown-superset in-distribution (P6); .sarib⇄JSON for structured output

### RA2: Doesn't beat Markdown on accuracy/tokens {.risk #ra2} ^ra2
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: pre-freeze
status:: watch
signal:: benchmarks flat vs Markdown

Interaction-efficiency thesis (D-002); measure (Rule 6)

### RA3: Format drifts out of pretraining distribution as it enriches {.risk #ra3} ^ra3
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: D
status:: open
signal:: richer constructs parse worse than prose

Keep surface Markdown-valid; quarantine extensions (P6/D-011)

### RA4: KV-cache benefits don't materialize (top-of-file churn) {.risk #ra4} ^ra4
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: C (Stage 9)
status:: mitigated
signal:: cache-hit rate low in agent loops

Append-only, deterministic order, stable prefix (P14)

### RA5: Derived-relation query cost too high → tempts materialization {.risk #ra5} ^ra5
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: B (Stage 6)
status:: mitigated
signal:: transitive-closure queries slow on big graphs

Mandatory traversal bounds (D-028) cap closure cost; provenance-marked caches (D-022)

### RA9: "Semantic traversal" wants an embedding signal not in the model → non-deterministic across tools {.risk #ra9} ^ra9
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: B (Stage 6)
status:: open
signal:: semantic-walk results differ per tool/model

Canonical form follows explicit semantic edges (`relates-to`/`refines`/`tag`) deterministically; embedding-ranked variant is explicitly non-canonical, tool-provided (D-026/D-029)

### RA10: Token-economy win shrinks for large-content nodes (`set-content` replaces whole node) {.risk #ra10} ^ra10
likelihood:: M
impact:: L
phase:: C (Stage 8)
status:: mitigated
signal:: big prose nodes cost content-sized edits

Keep nodes block-sized; intra-node splicing later (RM4); still ≪ document regeneration (D-002)

### RA11: Surface glyph tokenization unverified — chosen multi-char sigils (`::`,`[[`,`{#`,`^`) may fragment into many tokens {.risk #ra11} ^ra11
likelihood:: L
impact:: M
phase:: D (Stage 10)
status:: mitigated
signal:: grammar sigils cost more tokens than assumed

**Measured 2026-07-19** (`bench/tokenizer-report.md`): all load-bearing sigils single-token on o200k/cl100k/r50k; `{.`/`{#` = 2 (acceptable); B saves 26.9% whole-file vs A. Residual: re-run on open-weight tokenizers before freeze (G8)

### RA6: Model-version drift — tuned for 2026 models, degrades later {.risk #ra6} ^ra6
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: ongoing
status:: mitigated
signal:: new model gen parses format worse

Don't optimize vs tokenizers (D-002); ride durable distribution, not quirks

### RA7: Agent inference pollutes graph despite provenance {.risk #ra7} ^ra7
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: impl
status:: mitigated
signal:: inferred facts read as asserted

Agent writes = `inferred` by default; enforce assertion class (D-019)

### RA8: Legacy-prose enrichment cost (bulk extraction still expensive) {.risk #ra8} ^ra8
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: impl
status:: accepted
signal:: onboarding old corpora is slow/costly

Claim is *incremental write-time* structure, not bulk extraction; defer à la LazyGraphRAG ([ai-context.md](../research/ai-context.md))

## RD — Adoption & distribution {.section}

### RD1: Technically sound, socially dead {.risk #rd1} ^rd1
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: post-v1
status:: watch
signal:: no one emits .sarib

Carrier thesis (P6); ship consumers not just spec

### RD2: No consumer ships with v1 → dead letter {.risk #rd2} ^rd2
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: E (Stage 13)
status:: open
signal:: v1.0 has spec but no reader/writer

Reference MCP/CLI consumer is a v0.1 gate (S8)

### RD3: Markdown-superset constraint blocks a needed feature {.risk #rd3} ^rd3
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: D
status:: mitigated
signal:: a feature can't be expressed in valid Markdown

Extension mechanism (D-011); accept demotion to extension layer (D-006 reversal armed)

### RD4: A competing standard wins first (vendor blesses another format) {.risk #rd4} ^rd4
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: ongoing
status:: watch
signal:: a major agent stack standardizes elsewhere

Be a superset/interop layer, not a rival; move fast to reference impl; embeddable

### RD5: Network effects never start (chicken-and-egg) {.risk #rd5} ^rd5
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: post-v1
status:: mitigated
signal:: no tools ⇒ no files ⇒ no tools

Single guaranteed consumer = the user's own agent; per-file payoff (RH2 mitigation)

### RD6: JSON-as-the-format temptation — adopters treat the JSON interchange as *the* format, skipping the human surface {.risk #rd6} ^rd6
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: D→post-v1
status:: open
signal:: ecosystem emits/edits JSON, human surface unused

Canonical/interchange JSON is machine-only; Phase D delivers the pleasant author surface; position JSON as wire/at-rest, never what humans write (D-040)

## RG — Governance & ecosystem {.section}

### RG1: Governance vacuum / BDFL problem (the Markdown–CommonMark war) {.risk #rg1} ^rg1
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: E
status:: open
signal:: forks because no legitimate extension path

Explicit constitution at birth: who extends, what "conforming" means (P16; Stage 13+)

### RG2: Spec ambiguity → divergent parsers (Markdown's fate) {.risk #rg2} ^rg2
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: D/E
status:: open
signal:: same file, different parses across tools

Spec + conformance test suite from v0.1 (S6, P15/P16)

### RG3: Flavor fragmentation despite extension mechanism {.risk #rg3} ^rg3
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: post-v1
status:: mitigated
signal:: incompatible core extensions proliferate

Frozen core + self-announcing extensions + conformance brand (D-011, P16)

### RG4: Vocabulary fragmentation — competing community vocabularies {.risk #rg4} ^rg4
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: post-v1
status:: mitigated
signal:: three rival `task` vocabularies

Blessed standard library + namespacing + registry (D-021, D-025; Stage 13+)

### RG5: Extension namespace squatting / collision {.risk #rg5} ^rg5
likelihood:: L
impact:: M
phase:: post-v1
status:: open
signal:: prefix clashes between vendors

Namespace registry; registered/reverse-DNS prefixes (D-025)

### RG6: Vendor capture (embrace-extend-extinguish) {.risk #rg6} ^rg6
likelihood:: L
impact:: H
phase:: post-v1
status:: open
signal:: a platform ships an incompatible ".sarib+"

Open standard, permissive license, multi-implementer conformance (Stage 14/15)

## RL — Longevity & future-proofing {.section}

### RL1: Spec bloat over time violates the business-card target (S6) {.risk #rl1} ^rl1
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: E→post-v1
status:: mitigated
signal:: core grammar keeps growing

Freeze core; push features to vocab/extension; priority rule 4 (P16)

### RL2: Frozen core has a latent fatal flaw found post-freeze {.risk #rl2} ^rl2
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: post-freeze
status:: open
signal:: a common need is unrepresentable, no compat fix

Long critique cycles + reference impl + benchmarks *before* freeze; defined v2 migration story

### RL3: Dependency on transient tech (tokenizer, model API, a CRDT lib) {.risk #rl3} ^rl3
likelihood:: L
impact:: M
phase:: ongoing
status:: mitigated
signal:: format tied to one vendor's runtime

Model-agnostic (D-002); substrate = plain UTF-8 + git, not reinvented (L0)

### RL4: Project outlives / loses its maintainers {.risk #rl4} ^rl4
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: post-v1
status:: open
signal:: bus factor = 1; no independent impls

Self-hosting spec + conformance suite → independently implementable in a weekend (S6, RL4)

### RL5: The AI-native premise itself dates (context/tokenization paradigm shifts) {.risk #rl5} ^rl5
likelihood:: L
impact:: H
phase:: 5–10 yr
status:: accepted
signal:: agent architectures make the format's rationale moot

Hedge on durable value (human-readable plain-text graph) that survives even if AI specifics change

## RS — Security & safety {.section}

### RS1: Code execution / unsafe load (YAML tag-constructor RCE fate) {.risk #rs1} ^rs1
likelihood:: L
impact:: H
phase:: C/D
status:: mitigated
signal:: any construct triggers evaluation on load

Pure-data load, no executable directives (D-043)

### RS2: Expansion DoS — transclusion cycles / billion-laughs (YAML→K8s CVE) {.risk #rs2} ^rs2
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: C (Stage 9)
status:: mitigated
signal:: a small file expands unboundedly

Acyclic transclusion + cycle-detection + depth/size bound on resolution (D-043)

### RS3: Prompt injection via .sarib content (a file that instructs the reading agent) {.risk #rs3} ^rs3
likelihood:: H
impact:: H
phase:: impl
status:: open
signal:: file content steers an agent's behavior

Content is data, not instructions; agents treat .sarib as untrusted; no smuggled parser/model directives (Crockford-comments lesson, [standards-adoption.md](../research/standards-adoption.md))

### RS4: Provenance spoofing — forged `asserted-by`/human-claim {.risk #rs4} ^rs4
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: post-v1
status:: open
signal:: in-file provenance trusted as proof

Provenance is a *claim*, not proof; optional cryptographic signing as a companion spec; don't over-trust

### RS5: Reference-resolution exploit — engineered name collision redirects a link {.risk #rs5} ^rs5
likelihood:: L
impact:: M
phase:: D
status:: mitigated
signal:: link resolves to attacker-controlled node

Deterministic nearest-scope (D-024); explicit ids for sensitive refs; ambiguity diagnostics

### RS6: Canonicalization attacks — byte-forms that hash differently but mean the same {.risk #rs6} ^rs6
likelihood:: L
impact:: M
phase:: C (Stage 9)
status:: mitigated
signal:: signing/dedup inconsistent

Canonical form + canonicalizer rejects ambiguous encodings (dup keys/non-NFC/non-canonical numbers) (D-041/D-043)

## RP — Process & project {.section}

### RP1: Rediscovering RDF badly {.risk #rp1} ^rp1
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: A (Stage 2)
status:: mitigated
signal:: model converges on triples + ceremony

RDF-delta analysis done; kill-criterion (Rule 5) evaluated — no pivot needed (Stage 2 §3)

### RP2: Boil-the-ocean scope {.risk #rp2} ^rp2
likelihood:: H
impact:: M
phase:: ongoing
status:: mitigated
signal:: stages sprawl; no v1 boundary

v1 scope cap: core + op vocab + 3 projections; phase gates (charter)

### RP3: Efficiency claims unmeasured {.risk #rp3} ^rp3
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: pre-freeze
status:: open
signal:: "token efficient" asserted, never benchmarked

Benchmark harness before spec freeze (Rule 6; S2/S4/S7)

### RP4: Decisions accumulate contradictions (25+ entries) {.risk #rp4} ^rp4
likelihood:: M
impact:: M
phase:: ongoing
status:: mitigated
signal:: a new decision silently conflicts with an old one

Critique-first (Rule 1); reversal conditions; per-stage verification; priority order (Stage 3 §4)

### RP5: Analysis paralysis / never shipping {.risk #rp5} ^rp5
likelihood:: M
impact:: H
phase:: C→E
status:: open
signal:: endless refinement, no reference impl

Staged exit criteria; v1.0 is a defined endpoint (Stage 15); build reference impl in Phase E

### RP6: Resource/continuity interruption (credit limits, session loss) {.risk #rp6} ^rp6
likelihood:: H
impact:: L
phase:: ongoing
status:: mitigated
signal:: a session ends mid-stage (has happened)

Everything persisted to repo; README session log = resume point; self-contained stages
